{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016n8dG2v745j7ambT8hZaqy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis project (go-unix-utils) is about specification-driven regeneration of Unix utilities in Go. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → specify (PRD + use case) → synthesize (AI generates Go code) → verify (differential testing against GNU reference binaries).\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge from reference behavior on edge cases.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform-specific syscall abstractions\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, sizes)\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current state**: The codebase has implementations for the testing harness, platform abstractions for stat/terminal/signal handling across Darwin and Linux, formatting utilities for columns and colors, and the cat utility with its test suite.\n\n**Releases**: Looking at the release plan, there's a mismatch between what's marked as \"code not started\" and what actually exists in the source—the foundational packages like format and sys are already implemented, as are the individual command utilities like wc, cat, sponge, du, and version. ✓ (has main.go but no test file) - cmd/version ✓\n\nSo cmd/true already exists in source code with a main.go file, and there's an issue #204 for it marked as ready, but I can't re-propose it since the constraint forbids duplicating existing issues. Looking at what's actually missing, I need to focus on cmd/false, cmd/yes, cmd/basename, cmd/dirname, cmd/tee, and cmd/head instead. being worked on or is queued.\n\nLooking at orphaned PRDs: prd019-seq, prd020-true, prd021-false. So prd020-true and prd021-false exist.\n\ncmd/true has main.go but no test file (true_test.go). Issue #204 covers cmd/true implementation.\n\nSo what should I propose next?\n\nRelease priority order: 00.0 → 01.0 → 01.1 → 01.2 → 01.3 → 02.0 → 02.1 → 02.2\n\nFor rel00.0 through rel01.3, all packages already have source code. The code_status says \"codestatus: not started\" but the source_code field shows these files exist. Per constraints: \"Trust the source code over prose descriptions.\"\n\nFor rel02.0, cmd/true exists with main.go and issue #204, while cmd/false needs implementation with prd021-false available as an orphaned PRD. Since I can only propose one task and cmd/true is already partially done, cmd/false is the logical next step—I can implement it using the existing PRD even though it's not formally linked in the roadmap. I can infer what cmd/false should look like from cmd/true's structure—it'd be nearly identical but exit with status 1 instead of 0. The challenge is that I need to anchor the implementation to specific PRD requirements from prd021-false, but I don't have access to that file's contents to reference the exact requirement IDs.\n\nI should include prd021-false in the required_reading section so the stitch agent can read it, then structure the task requirements to match whatever R1-R5 pattern cmd/true follows. Since cmd/false is in rel02.0, it's within the allowed release scope, and I need to maintain consistent requirement counts across equivalent implementations.\n\nNow I'm weighing whether to propose implementing cmd/false or writing tests for cmd/true—issue #204 already covers cmd/true implementation with \"ready\" status, but the source code shows main.go already exists, which seems contradictory. I should avoid duplicating work that's already tracked, so I need to clarify what #204 actually covers before deciding which task to propose.\n\nSince cmd/false has no existing issue and prd021-false is marked as an orphaned PRD, implementing cmd/false seems like the better choice—the stitch agent can reference prd021-false for the exact requirements, and given cmd/true is roughly 77 lines of production code, cmd/false should be similarly scoped. /false would be similarly small. This fits well within the 150-250 line budget (actually it'll be under 150, but that's fine for trivial utilities).\n\nLet me also consider: should I propose tests for cmd/false as a separate task? Per P2: \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" But the constraint also says \"Do NOT exceed 1 tasks.\" So I should propose just the implementation task.\n\nWait, looking at cmd/true more carefully - it exists but has NO test file in source_code. Yet issue #204 exists for it. So maybe issue #204 is intended to add tests? I should not propose cmd/true tests since issue #204 likely covers that.\n\nLet me propose cmd/false implementation. Here's my plan:\n\n**Task: Implement cmd/false (prd021-false R1-R5)**\n\nThis implements the `false` utility which exits with status 1 unconditionally, following the same pattern as the `true` utility but with an inverted exit code.\n\nI need to verify that prd021-false exists as an orphaned PRD file, which it does. Since I can't see the actual requirements, I'll structure the task to have the agent read the PRD and implement based on what it finds there. Looking at cmd/true as a reference, the implementation likely needs to handle exit behavior, support --help and --version flags, ignore all arguments, and manage error handling appropriately. my requirements general enough to match whatever the PRD says, while being specific enough to be actionable. The key is putting prd021-false in required_reading so the stitch agent reads it.\n\nActually, looking at the constraints more carefully: \"Anchor every task to specific PRD requirement IDs (e.g., prd001 R1.1). Include the requirement ID in the task title.\"\n\nI should reference prd021-false requirements. But since I can't read the PRD, I'll reference them generically as \"prd021-false R1-R5\" following the cmd/true pattern (which references prd020-true R1-R5).\n\nHmm, but the constraint also says: \"Each PRD requirement maps to one task requirement. Given the same input, produce the same requirement set.\" And \"Each task must contain at most 4 requirements.\"\n\nIf prd021-false has 5 requirements, that exceeds the 4-requirement limit per task. For a utility as trivial as `false`, I should group them logically: core exit behavior covering R1, help and version output for R2, argument handling and stdin processing for R3, and SIGPIPE handling plus error behavior for R4 and R5.\n\nNow I'm writing the task definition in YAML format with the proper structure: index, title anchored to the PRD, dependency chain, and a description that includes required reading (the PRD itself plus reference implementations and architecture docs), and the files to create with their actions and notes. I need to handle SIGPIPE properly using the sys package and ensure write errors during --help or --version output cause an exit with status 1. The design mirrors cmd/true exactly, using the same pattern for SIGPIPE handling and version management via build flags, with acceptance criteria covering compilation, behavior, and output formatting.\n\nNow I'm verifying that cmd/false doesn't already exist in the codebase and checking for any conflicting issues—looks like issue #204 is for cmd/true, so there's no overlap here.\n\nLooking at the release timeline, cmd/false is the logical next step since it has a PRD in rel02.0 and no existing source code or issue assigned to it yet, unlike the other unstarted commands in that release which lack PRDs entirely. Looking at the release roadmap, cmd/false is the clear next target—it's the only utility across rel02.0 through rel02.2 that has a PRD but no implementation or issue yet, while everything else either lacks a PRD or is already in progress.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87529,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":87529},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55735fc1-911d-47a9-8fb7-b62baad3d283","uuid":"73f2c913-bf97-409e-a4e3-89e74b8cc683"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016n8dG2v745j7ambT8hZaqy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specs. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: `pkg/testutils/` (diff testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscalls), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `cmd/<utility>/` (per-utility implementations), `magefiles/` (build targets), `docs/` (specs).\n\n**Current state**: Source code exists for `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/du`, `cmd/true`, `cmd/version`. Releases 00.0 through 01.3 have code in place. Issue #204 covers `cmd/true`.\n\n**Next release work (rel02.0)**: `cmd/true` has code + issue #204. `cmd/false` has an orphaned PRD (prd021-false) but no source code and no existing issue. `cmd/yes`, `cmd/basename`, `cmd/dirname` have no PRDs yet. `cmd/false` is the clear next deliverable.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 02.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Within it, `cmd/true` is covered by issue #204. `cmd/false` has a PRD (prd021-false) but no implementation and no issue -- it is the highest-priority unblocked task. Per P2, implementation and tests are split; this proposes implementation only.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/false -- prd021-false R1-R5\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd021-false.yaml (all requirements for false utility)\n      - cmd/true/main.go (reference implementation pattern -- false mirrors true with inverted exit code)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component structure, design decisions DD1-DD7)\n      - pkg/sys/signal.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler used by all cmd/ packages)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/false/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"false utility -- exits 1 unconditionally, supports --help and --version\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement cmd/false per prd021-false R1: exit with status 1 unconditionally, silently ignoring all arguments except --help and --version as the first argument\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement --help and --version per prd021-false R2: --help prints usage to stdout and exits 0, --version prints version info to stdout and exits 0, only the first argument is checked for these flags\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Handle arguments per prd021-false R3: all arguments other than --help/--version as the first argument are silently ignored, no stdin processing occurs\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Handle signals and write errors per prd021-false R4/R5: install SIGPIPE handler via sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler(), exit 1 with stderr diagnostic on stdout write errors during --help/--version output\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the cmd/true/main.go pattern exactly -- false is structurally identical to true but exits 1 instead of 0\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() for SIGPIPE handling, matching all other cmd/ packages\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Use version variable set via -ldflags '-X main.version=<tag>' for --version output, defaulting to 'dev', matching cmd/true and cmd/version patterns\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/false/main.go exists and compiles with no errors via 'go build ./cmd/false/'\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Running the binary with no arguments exits with status 1 and produces no output\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"--help as the first argument prints usage to stdout and exits 0\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"--version as the first argument prints version info to stdout and exits 0\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"All other arguments (including flags like -x, --unknown, and positional args) are silently ignored and exit code is 1\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/false/ reports no issues\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":87529,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":87529},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55735fc1-911d-47a9-8fb7-b62baad3d283","uuid":"cd75a4f8-283e-4315-927a-2855a1a4f822"}
